  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Saturday)\nIssued at 1231 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nStrong and gusty north winds will prevail behind the cold front\nSunday, and post-frontal low cloud may persist for longer than\nwhat models indicate. The data supports sub-advisory level gusts \nin the 30s across NE OK. The timing of the front warranted\nadjustments to the hourly temp forecast away from the typical\ndiurnal curve. Temps may struggle to warm much above what they are\nin the morning in some areas. After suggesting that there may be\nsome potential for afternoon storm development on the front over\nthe far eastern portions of the forecast area yesterday, the RRFS\nand HRRR for that matter both show any such potential to be well\neast of the area by Sunday afternoon.\n\nCooler than average weather will persist into early next week, as\ncool Canadian high pressure settles down into the central part of\nthe country. A more widespread hard freeze is expected Monday and\nTuesday nights. However, today marks the end of the defined\ngrowing season for our area. Thus, no frost/freeze headlines will\nbe issued.\n\nAn upper wave is expected to traverse across the south-central\nCONUS late Tuesday into Wednesday. This system will have limited\nmoisture to work with and the bulk of the forcing is expected to\ntrack from TX and into AR/LA. Measurable precipitation is not \nexpected in our area with this system.\n\nAfter perusing a conglomeration of ensemble and new 12Z\ndeterministic model data, it appears that the upcoming holiday and\nholiday weekend should be free of weather worries across the\nforecast area. A wave is expected to traverse across the region \naround Thanksgiving night and may bring a chance for precipitation\nas we get closer, but even if it does it is not expected to be of\nthe frozen variety.\n\nLacy",
